1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,500
Most AI coding assistants operate unconstrained: they suggest code that looks plausible but fails silently,

2
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:18,500
overwrite test assertions to fake green results, or loop indefinitely on runtime errors.

3
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:24,824
BenchProof treats code generation as a guarded engineering process, powered by NVIDIA Nemotron on Nebius Token Factory.

4
00:00:24,824 --> 00:00:33,000
Here we select the StringCalculator project. Notice the strict boundary:

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,500
Application code is editable, while test files in tests are completely protected and immutable.

6
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,810
This prevents the model from weakening tests. We submit our task brief to fix negative number handling.

7
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:54,500
Before modifying any file, the agent inspects the code and queries NVIDIA Nemotron to generate a plan.

8
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:03,861
We review the plan steps and approve it with an immutable cryptographic plan hash. No mutation occurs without approval.

9
00:01:03,861 --> 00:01:13,000
The task runner executes a baseline check in an isolated Docker container, confirming the failing assertion.

10
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Next, NVIDIA Nemotron generates a surgical edit. The candidate is re-tested in a fresh container with network denied.

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,391
All tests pass with strict memory limits and process isolation, reaching Review Ready.

12
00:01:27,391 --> 00:01:35,500
We can inspect the unified diff showing the exact changes made to raise ValueError with the negative numbers.

13
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:43,602
Every state mutation is recorded in a CAS checkpoint log, allowing instant rollback without resetting budget limits.

14
00:01:43,602 --> 00:01:53,000
Finally, we export the complete verifiable evidence bundle: a signed ZIP archive containing the diff and test logs.

15
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:03,278
BenchProof is deployed and accessible live at benchproof.vercel.app, delivering reliable, guarded autonomous coding.
